    <small|<with|color|dark grey|<math|J>: large-field functional, <math|E>: energy (both Lecture<nbsp>7); <math|b<rsup|<math-up|b>>,b<rsup|<math-up|l>>>: bounded- and large-field parts of the drift.>>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Why not Polchinski with a frozen cube?>

    \;

    <strong|Programme:> keep <math|-<frac|\<lambda\>|3>\<varphi\><rsup|3>>
    unclipped, clip the higher orders (result: <math|f>), pay in the source of <math|R=F-f>.
    Test it on one site: <math|K<rsub|q>=q>, <math|S=1/q>.

    <\equation*>
      F<rsub|q><around|(|x|)>=-<frac|\<lambda\>|3>\<bbb-E\><rsub|x>\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>><rsup|3>=q<around|(|m<rsub|q><around|(|x|)>-x|)>,<space|1em><around|\||m<rsub|q><around|(|x|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>2<around|(|\<lambda\>/3|)><rsup|-1/4>+2<around|(|3q<around|\||x|\|>/\<lambda\>|)><rsup|1/3>.
    </equation*>

