    <small|<with|color|dark grey|Each step inserts the previous correction
    into the loop: one more loop, one more <math|g<rsub|q>>. Two steps give a
    defect of order <math|g<rsub|q><rsup|3>>.>>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Closing the mass path>

    Given a trial <strong|scalar> path <math|\<mu\>>, compute the two steps
    of the iteration, corrections and centred kernels, then

    <\equation*>
      \<beta\><rsub|q><around|[|\<mu\>|]>=<around|\<langle\>|D\<cal-T\><rsub|q><around|[|M<rsup|<around|[|3|]>><around|[|\<mu\>|]>,\<ell\><rsup|<around|[|3|]>><around|[|\<mu\>|]>|]><around|(|0|)>|\<rangle\>>,<space|2em>\<cal-A\><around|[|\<mu\>|]><rsub|q>=b<rsub|\<ast\>>+<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>><rsup|q>\<beta\><rsub|s><around|[|\<mu\>|]>\<mathd\>s.
    </equation*>

    <\myframed>
      The mass is the <strong|fixed point> <math|\<mu\>=\<cal-A\><around|[|\<mu\>|]>>:
      a scalar function of <math|q>, not an operator-valued path. It is the
      only fixed point we solve; the centred kernels are explicit outputs of
      two Picard steps.
    </myframed>

    This is an equation for the whole path: the corrections and centred
    kernels at <math|q> depend on <math|\<mu\>> at later scales.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|What the fixed point gives>

    \;

    For <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>> large, uniformly in <math|N>:

    <\itemize>
      <item>a unique scalar path in the ball <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>R>
      (norm on the next slide), so <math|<around|\||\<mu\><rsub|q>|\|>\<lesssim\>\<lambda\>
      q<rsup|1/2>>;

      <item>centred kernels: <math|v<rsub|1><around|(|\<Theta\><rsup|<around|[|j|]>><rsub|q>|)>\<lesssim\>\<lambda\><rsup|2>q<rsup|-1/2>>;

      <item>increments: <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|k,q><around|(|\<ell\><rsup|<around|[|j|]>>-\<ell\><rsup|<around|[|j-1|]>>|)>\<lesssim\>g<rsub|q><rsup|j>q*r<rsub|q><rsup|2-k>>,
      <math|j=1,2,3>, <math|4\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>12-2j>, where

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-N\><rsub|k,q><around|(|f|)>=<math-ss|S><rsub|k><around|(|f|)>+<sqrt|q>v<rsub|1><around|(|D<rsup|k-1>f<around|(|0|)>|)>;
      </equation*>

      <item>defect: <math|<math-ss|S><rsub|4><around|(|\<cal-D\><rsup|o><rsub|q>|)>\<lesssim\>\<lambda\><rsup|5>q<rsup|-3>>,
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-E\><rsup|c><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|row>>\<lesssim\>g<rsub|q><rsup|4>>.
    </itemize>

    <\myframed>
      <strong|Explicit counterterm.> <math|\<mu\><rsub|N>=r-\<lambda\><math-up|Tr>
      C+<frac|2|3>\<lambda\><rsup|2><big|int> C<rsup|3>> is reached by a
      unique boundary value <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>,N>>, and
      <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>,N>> converges as <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>. The
      measure does not depend on <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>.
    </myframed>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Two distinct iterations>

    <strong|Inner recursion:> for each trial scalar path <math|\<mu\>>,
    start from the seed and compute exactly two steps.

    <\equation*>
      \<mu\>\<longmapsto\><around|(|\<ell\><rsup|<around|[|2|]>>,\<Theta\><rsup|<around|[|2|]>>|)><around|[|\<mu\>|]>\<longmapsto\><around|(|\<ell\><rsup|<around|[|3|]>>,\<Theta\><rsup|<around|[|3|]>>|)><around|[|\<mu\>|]>\<longmapsto\>\<beta\><around|[|\<mu\>|]>\<longmapsto\>\<cal-A\><around|[|\<mu\>|]>.
    </equation*>

    <strong|Outer contraction:> start at <math|\<mu\><rsup|<around|(|0|)>><rsub|q>=b<rsub|\<ast\>>>;
    iterate <math|\<mu\><rsup|<around|(|n+1|)>>=\<cal-A\><around|[|\<mu\><rsup|<around|(|n|)>>|]>>,
    in the norm <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<ast\>>=sup<rsub|q\<geqslant\>q<rsub|\<ast\>>><around|\||\<mu\><rsub|q>|\|>/<around|(|\<lambda\><sqrt|q>|)>>:

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-A\><around|[|\<mu\>|]>-\<cal-A\><around|[|<wide|\<mu\>|^>|]>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<ast\>>\<leqslant\>C*g<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>><around|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>-<wide|\<mu\>|^>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<ast\>>.
    </equation*>

    <\myframed>
      Every outer step recomputes the same two-level force. Its depth stays
      <math|2>, so no additional field derivatives are consumed. At the
      fixed point the scalar linear flow is exact; the defect, nonlinear and
      centred linear, remains.
    </myframed>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Mass differences gain a small insertion>

    Compare two scalar paths with the same <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>>,
    <math|d=<around|\<\|\|\>|\<mu\>-<wide|\<mu\>|^>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<ast\>>>;
    <math|\<delta\>> denotes output differences,
    <math|\<delta\>M<rsup|<around|[|j|]>>=\<delta\>\<mu\>I+\<delta\>\<Theta\><rsup|<around|[|j|]>>>.

    <\equation*>
      R<rsup|<around|[|j|]>>-<wide|R|^><rsup|<around|[|j|]>>=-R<rsup|<around|[|j|]>><around|(|\<delta\>M<rsup|<around|[|j|]>>+D\<delta\>\<ell\><rsup|<around|[|j|]>>|)><wide|R|^><rsup|<around|[|j|]>>.
    </equation*>

    The relative scalar and centred insertions obey

    <\equation*>
      <frac|<around|\||\<delta\>\<mu\><rsub|q>|\|>|q>\<leqslant\>g<rsub|q>d,<space|2em>\<eta\><rsub|q><around|(|\<delta\>\<Theta\><rsup|<around|[|j|]>>|)>\<lesssim\>g<rsub|q><rsup|3>d,
    </equation*>

    the latter by integrating the differences of the centred sources from
    infinity. Loop comparisons through the two levels give
    <math|\<cal-N\><rsub|k,q><around|(|\<delta\>\<ell\><rsup|<around|[|j|]>>|)>\<lesssim\>g<rsub|q><rsup|2>q*r<rsub|q><rsup|2-k>d>,
    <math|j=2,3>, <math|4\<leqslant\>k\<leqslant\>12-2j> (the seed does not depend on <math|\<mu\>>).

    <\myframed>
      The resulting source differences are

      <\equation*>
        <around|\||\<delta\>\<beta\><rsub|q>|\|>\<lesssim\>\<lambda\><rsup|2>q<rsup|-1>d,<space|2em>v<rsub|1><around|(|\<delta\><around|(|B<rsup|<around|[|j|]>><rsub|q>|)><rsup|\<circ\>>|)>\<lesssim\>\<lambda\><rsup|3>q<rsup|-2>d.
      </equation*>
    </myframed>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Integration leaves the factor <math|g<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>>>

    <strong|Scalar update:> the common boundary value <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>> cancels.
    With <math|x=q/q<rsub|\<ast\>>\<geqslant\>1>,

    <\equation*>
      <around|\||\<delta\>\<cal-A\><around|[|\<mu\>|]><rsub|q>|\|>\<leqslant\>C\<lambda\><rsup|2>d<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>><rsup|q><frac|\<mathd\>s|s>=C\<lambda\><rsup|2>log x*d,
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      <frac|<around|\||\<delta\>\<cal-A\><around|[|\<mu\>|]><rsub|q>|\|>|\<lambda\><sqrt|q>>\<leqslant\>C*g<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>><frac|log x|<sqrt|x>>d\<leqslant\><frac|2C|\<mathe\>>g<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>d.
    </equation*>

    The centred kernels are not part of the unknown: their variation is
    already contained in <math|\<delta\>\<beta\>>.

    <\myframed>
      Contraction constant <math|C*g<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>>. Fix an
      invariant ball, then choose <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>> so that
      <math|C*g<rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>\<less\>1/2>, uniformly in <math|N>.
    </myframed>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Why the derivative budget stays fixed>

    A loop needs <math|k+2> legs to control a <math|k>-leg vertex.
    Every evaluation of <math|\<cal-A\>> uses the same budget:

    <\equation*>
      \<ell\><rsup|<around|[|1|]>><text|: >10\<longrightarrow\>\<ell\><rsup|<around|[|2|]>><text|: >8\<longrightarrow\>\<ell\><rsup|<around|[|3|]>><text|: >6\<longrightarrow\><text|defect: >4.
    </equation*>

    <small|A <math|k>-leg force vertex has <math|k-1> field derivatives.
    The mass source uses only the four-leg vertex of <math|\<ell\><rsup|<around|[|3|]>>>,
    the centred sources those of <math|\<ell\><rsup|<around|[|1|]>>>, <math|\<ell\><rsup|<around|[|2|]>>>.>

    <\itemize>
      <item>A path difference inserts <math|\<delta\>M<rsup|<around|[|j|]>>> in a resolvent.
      It is field-independent: <math|D<rsub|y>\<delta\>M<rsup|<around|[|j|]>>=0>. The comparison
      uses the same field-derivative ranges as the construction.

      <item><math|\<cal-A\>> returns a <strong|scalar path>. At the next outer
      step, restart from the original seed and perform the same two steps.
      We do not use the previous <math|\<ell\><rsup|<around|[|3|]>>> as a new seed.
    </itemize>

    <\myframed>
      The outer iteration contracts in one fixed space of scalar paths.
      The finite Lipschitz estimates transfer convergence to the
      corrections; their derivative budget does not shrink with the outer index.
    </myframed>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Two mass renormalisations>

    \;

    The two divergent self-energies of Appendix<nbsp>A.2 of Lecture<nbsp>5:

    <\itemize>
      <item><strong|Tadpole>: <math|-\<lambda\>*<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>><rsup|\<infty\>><math-up|Tr>R<rsub|0,q><rsup|2><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>q\<approx\>-\<lambda\><math-up|Tr>C\<sim\>-\<lambda\>N>.
      Wick ordering: <math|<frac|\<lambda\>|12>*\<varphi\><rsup|4>-<frac|1|2>*\<lambda\>*c*\<varphi\><rsup|2>=<frac|\<lambda\>|12>*H<rsub|4>*<around|(|\<varphi\>;c|)>+>const.

      <item><strong|Sunset>: contracting the bubble in
      <math|D<rsup|3>\<ell\><rsup|<around|[|3|]>><around|(|0|)>> with <math|R<rsub|0><rsup|2>> and
      integrating gives the two-loop self-energy,

      <\equation*>
        <frac|2|3>*\<lambda\><rsup|2>*<big|int>C<rsup|3>\<approx\><frac|\<lambda\><rsup|2>|24*\<pi\><rsup|2>>*log
        N<space|1em><around|(|=2*\<gamma\><rsub|T>*<text| of Lecture 5>|)>.
      </equation*>
    </itemize>

    Power counting: nothing else. In the flow: the integrals from
    <math|q=\<infty\>> of all vertices with <math|k\<geqslant\>4> legs
    converge; only the constant mode of the linear jet needs a boundary condition
    at a finite scale.

    <math|\<mu\><rsub|q>> at fixed <math|q> has a limit as
    <math|N\<to\>\<infty\>>; <math|\<mu\><rsub|N>> does not. <strong|Never
    interchange the two limits.>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
